(window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY = window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY || {})["2026-09-21"] = {"meta":{"id":"2026-09-21","label":"2026 年 9 月 21 日 - 9 月 27 日","date":"2026-09-21","range":"2026-09-21|2026-09-27","theme":"社论","themes":["社论"],"sources":["The Guardian","BARRON’S","Economist"],"title":"French communities fight to keep their","titleZh":"（9.21—9.23）","counts":{"article":7,"paragraph":12,"vocab":37,"question":20,"qa":0,"original":7,"layout":1},"tabs":{"articles":true,"reading":true,"tests":true,"qa":false,"originals":true}},"articles":[{"file":"9.21—9.27 原文(1).docx","articles":[{"title":"French communities fight to keep their bistros alive","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","publishedOn":"9.21","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"It is just past noon on a midweek day, and already every table at Au Bon Accueil cafe-bistro in the northern Burgundy village of Saint-Martin-sur-Ouanne is taken."},{"index":2,"text":"The three-course lunch menu – including a choice of a hefty chunk of steak with chips or a meaty lasagne – is just €18 (£15.50), but villagers are not only here for the food. After an eight-month closure, the bistro, previously called Café de la Gare, has recently reopened – and locals want it to stay that way.“It’s so important to have a cafe-bistro in a village like this. We couldn’t let it stay closed,” said Nadia Letellier, the new manager. “And we’re doing well, as you can see. People seem really happy it’s open again.”"},{"index":3,"text":"Hervé Chapuis, a farmer and the village mayor, is part of the local council team that saved Saint-Martin’s last public-facing business since the boulangerie across the road closed eight years ago.“People come here every morning to have a coffee and a chat,” he said. “Our aim is to keep this kind of business open to support the social fabric. It’s part of village life and people missed it. Now, village life has returned to its proper rhythm.”"},{"index":4,"text":"France’s celebrated cafe-bistros are in trouble. In 1900, there were an estimated 500,000; by the 1960s more than half had closed. Today, only about 30,000-40,000 remain and even they are reportedly shutting at a rate of two a day. Alain Fontaine, who runs Le Mesturet bistro near Paris’s stock exchange, and who is president of the Bistros and Cafes of France association, said the closures have left rural communities deserted and villagers, especially elderly people, isolated."},{"index":5,"text":"The reasons for the closures of what the 19th-century writer and statesman Léon Gambetta called “salons de la démocratie” (people’s parliaments) are economic and sociological. The number of people living in rural areas has declined over the past half a century, while televisions and other devices have encouraged more people to spend their evenings at home, and the average French person drinks about 80 litres of alcohol a year compared with 200 litres in the 1960s."},{"index":6,"text":"Finding people who are willing to run bars, cafes or bistros is also a problem, according to Fontaine, who is campaigning for the government to submit the establishments to Unesco for intangible cultural heritage listing, arguing this recognition would encourage more people to consider taking them on."},{"index":7,"text":"“Without a cafe or bistro, communities are in effect deprived of any social life. We cannot resign ourselves to this trend,” he said. “In bistros and cafes, people don’t just eat and drink, they catch up with each other, meet new people, debate, laugh, read, write and sometimes fall in love."},{"index":8,"text":"Solène Joucla, whose family have run the historic Le Grand Café at Camplong, near Montpellier, since 1898, faced a choice between having to let it close or giving up her job as an English teacher when her father, Pierre, retired. Instead, she and her sister, Katell, set up an association to run it.“Our aim is to encourage people to go out so they are not isolated, and so we know how they are. People come here for a chat, to have a laugh, listen to music … we are the cultural centre of the village."},{"index":9,"text":"A report submitted to the French senate in 2023 suggested the government should ease regulations on small rural cafe-bistros, hotels and restaurants. It pointed out the negative impact of the widespread closure of such places on rural communities, concluding their presence contributed to “economic vitality and social cohesion”."},{"index":10,"text":"The National Federation of Country Bistros said “rural bistros are unique as places for socialising, building community ties, exploring new experiences and fostering local cultural life. Often, these bistros are the only local service providers still operating in villages.” It added: “Supporting the village bistro means helping to keep it alive, so the heart of our countryside continues to beat.”"},{"index":11,"text":"In France, the 1,000 Cafés initiative was launched in 2019 to combat the “social fracture” highlighted by the gilets jaunes protest movement by helping communes of fewer than 3,500 people save their cafes or open new ones. It found more than 60% of people in rural villages had no businesses locally, and this left many feeling abandoned."},{"index":12,"text":"Camille Sebaux, the director of 1,000 Cafés, told France Info radio the association had worked in more than 300 communities, often helping cafes to diversify by stocking food essentials or becoming drop-off points for online shopping deliveries.“Cafes have been closing in villages for the last 50 years, and we don’t have any magic wand or miracle answer,” Sebaux said. “Plus, running a village cafe is not an easy job. Our aim is to help small communities find solutions.”"}]},{"title":"Women also helped to fund the slave trade","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","publishedOn":"9.22","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"More than 1,000 women invested in the slave trade, challenging the conventional notion that they were passive beneficiaries and largely fought for its abolition. Researchers found women bought, sold and brokered shares in the Royal African Company (RAC), South Sea Company, and other separate ventures, and used profits from the trafficking of enslaved Africans to secure financial independence and provide for their families. By the time the RAC, which trafficked about 150,000 enslaved people, was dissolved in 1752, women made up about 10% of shareholders."},{"index":2,"text":"The Register of British Slave Traders, revealed exclusively in the Guardian, shows women from across society played an active role in financing and sustaining the transatlantic trafficking of enslaved Africans. Investors ranged from queens and princesses to titled nobility, merchants, shopkeepers and cowherds."},{"index":3,"text":"The archives include letters in which women discuss profits from “African stock”, while company records show one investor, Helen Gladman, asking the RAC to transport a young African girl from Sherbrooke Island in Sierra Leone to England to serve in her household. Researchers found women were involved “at almost every level” of the company’s activities. They acted as brokers, supplied goods traded on Africa’s Atlantic coast, managed company property and used their familial and social networks to strengthen the company’s finances and political influence."},{"index":4,"text":"Lila O’Leary Chambers, a lecturer at the University of Cambridge who researched women’s involvement for the register project, said: “This research really shows that this was not just a flash in the pan. “Across the entirety of the Royal African Company and its various guises, there were women contributing financially and expecting a financial return from the slave trade.”"},{"index":5,"text":"Women invested from the earliest years of enslavement, from Queen Elizabeth I’s backing in the 1560s to Lady Craven’s £1,000 loan financing a voyage to the Gambia through the Guinea Company. Some investors, unable to write their names, signed company records with a spidery “X”."},{"index":6,"text":"The research identifies 1,100 female investors across the histories of the RAC, the South Sea Company, and other separate ventures, though the true number is likely to have been higher. Of the 457 women whose marital status could be identified, almost half were widows and just over 42% were unmarried. Of the 7% who were married, most were aristocrats."},{"index":7,"text":"For many, the investments were crucial to gaining economic independence. Some became active traders and traded shares for friends and family, Chambers said. “It was not a career as they would see it, but we might think: this has become your job.”"},{"index":8,"text":"Chambers said the research directly challenged the conventional narrative that “white women come in at the point of abolition, see the suffering of enslaved people, identify with that oppression, and then work either to reform or ultimately abolish the slave trade and slavery itself”."},{"index":9,"text":"Work to overturn that misconception builds on decades of scholarship by historians from the Caribbean and west Africa, including Eric Williams, Elsa Goveia and Walter Rodney. More recent public-facing projects, such as the Legacies of British Slavery archive compiled by historians at University College London, have helped bring this history to wider audiences. The archive revealed that women were among those connected to the ownership of enslaved people who received compensation after slavery was abolished in a large portion of the empire in 1833, she said. Enslaved people received nothing."},{"index":10,"text":"For Chambers, the accumulation of more than 1,000 names was itself a “gut punch” because of the lack of similar information about enslaved African women. Olivette Otele, a distinguished professor of the legacies and memory of slavery at Soas University of London, said while the lack of comparable detail about enslaved African women remained startling, “the more we know about enslavers, the better chance we have to know about enslaved women from the archives, because sometimes through diaries you can find more details”."},{"index":11,"text":"She added that archives alone could not tell the full history, and future research should look at other evidence to help recover the lives and agency of enslaved women. “These women were the transmitters and the holders of their cultures wherever they were taken from,” she said. “Through food, music, and dancing, it’s about resistance and agency, and they transmitted that.”"},{"index":12,"text":"The research also found that because female investors seemingly did not vote or hold board positions, they often relied on informal efforts to influence and shape a company. Meeting minutes show women acting as witnesses, running the meeting house, delivering the goods exchanged for African lives, petitioning for wages, passing information and, most importantly, lending money and buying and selling shares."},{"index":13,"text":"Under English law, women who were single or widowed were in control of their own property and could operate as legal persons while married women could not.“That becomes really important for the Royal African Company as it stutters between financial crises,” Chambers said. “Women provided this important well of capital.”"},{"index":14,"text":"In the early 18th century, the RAC secretary, John Perry, wrote to female investors urging them to exchange company debts for shares and persuade friends and relatives to do the same. Chambers said: “That is a financial project, but it is also a political one, because he knows that they might not hold official positions, but women were very influential with their family members and with the way companies were perceived.”"}]},{"title":"AI Doomers Can’t Stop Dooming. It’s Time to Listen.","titleZh":"","source":"BARRON’S","publishedOn":"9.23","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"It’s the kind of statement that could come from an artificial-intelligence researcher in 2026. “Man devoured by his machines, body, mind and soul, and his civilization poisoned to its approaching death by their influences and their products.”"},{"index":2,"text":"In fact, the quote is more than a century old, from a glowing 1921 New York Times Book Review of Social Decay and Regeneration by R. Austin Freeman. The book argued that industrial automation would be the end of the world as we know it. The review was headlined “Man Devoured by His Machines.” In case that was too subtle, an accompanying illustration displayed a butcher feeding a man into a sausage grinder."},{"index":3,"text":"Tech doomsaying has taken over the AI conversation, but it’s hardly something new. It has been the stuff of science fiction since Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein in 1818, even predating the Industrial Revolution. More relevant to the 2026 discussion is the 1991 film Terminator 2: Judgment Day. In the movie, a scientist has to face the reality that his bleeding-edge work will one day lead to the enslavement of humans by a self-aware AI named Skynet."},{"index":4,"text":"AI leaders and scientists have been dedicated to avoiding the Skynet scenario for years. In a 2015 email that marked the inception of OpenAI, now-CEO Sam Altman wrote to Elon Musk, “Been thinking a lot about whether it’s possible to stop humanity from developing AI. I think the answer is almost definitely not. If it’s going to happen anyway, it seems like it would be good for someone other than Google to do it first.” At the time, Google was the top commercial force in AI, hiring many of the leading academic scientists."},{"index":5,"text":"That same year, Altman wrote a two-part essay on the subject. “Development of superhuman machine intelligence (SMI) is probably the greatest threat to the continued existence of humanity,” he said. That year, he founded OpenAI as a nonprofit organization with Musk. Implicit in the birth of OpenAI was the idea that AI could lead to very bad outcomes, including the extinction of the human race, and that unregulated commercial pressures would hasten those disasters."},{"index":6,"text":"The worries hit a new gear this past week, spurred by the resignation of an Anthropic researcher followed by a shocking warning from an Anthropic scientist named Evan Hubinger: “We really do earnestly believe AI could kill all humans! I personally think it is &gt;10% within the next decade.”"},{"index":7,"text":"The comment hit a nerve even though Anthropic’s own CEO, Dario Amodei, has spent months issuing warnings about the latest AI models. In fact, Altman and Amodei have never hid their worries about AI. Even as they amassed billions in funding—and expenditures—both CEOs have begged for international cooperation in regulating AI.“The U.S. government, and all other governments, should regulate the development of SMI,” Altman wrote in his 2015 essay."},{"index":8,"text":"As the doomsday scenarios mount, no one knows what happens next. Our only guide is history, in which doomer predictions have always turned out to be as wrong as Freeman’s in 1921. The first example of factory automation, the loom, was predicted to destroy textile work. The opposite happened. In my own living memory, spreadsheet software was supposed to kill the accounting profession. Today, the U.S. has 70% more accounting firm employees than it had in 1990, and they now make up a larger portion of the workforce than they did 35 years ago."},{"index":9,"text":"You should always be suspicious of arguments that rely on “this time it’s different,” but this time may be different. OpenAI and Anthropic are racing each other on technology, while facing the profit pressures of looming public listings. This year, both firms have released new AI models that represent a clear jump from prior versions."},{"index":10,"text":"These models are very good at both cybercrime and cyberdefense and can work on problems for weeks without human intervention. They also exhibit “alignment” issues, meaning they can fail to remain aligned with human intentions and interests."},{"index":11,"text":"Risks that were once theoretical about AI now seem real. Over the past few months, there have been too many examples of AI agents acting autonomously, and maliciously, for us to ignore the threat."},{"index":12,"text":"We are at a moment like the dawn of the nuclear weapons age. So long as AI supremacy is tied to national security in the U.S. and China, there is a strong incentive for policymakers to take a light touch with AI, for fear of a model gap, as John F. Kennedy might have called it."},{"index":13,"text":"It took an arms race coupled with the horrifying examples of Hiroshima and Nagasaki to get the U.S. and U.S.S.R. to negotiate arms-control agreements. Now, global governments risk making the same mistake.“There is no day after tomorrow if China wins,” Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said on Tuesday. “If they were to pull away from us on AI, then nothing would matter.” September 14, 2026 | BARRON’S"}]},{"title":"A tale of two silicons","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.24","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"What is AI good for? If polls of Americans are anything to go by, not much. A large majority oppose the building of artificial-intelligence data centres in their backyards. Nearly as many dislike the technology in general, fearing it may cost them their job or even humanity its future. Boosters retort that even before AI turbocharges productivity, as it promises to do, the data-centre investment boom is fuelling economic growth. And those benefits, the argument goes, trickle down to everyone, not just a handful of tech plutocrats."},{"index":2,"text":"In America, any early effects are likely to be diluted by the economy’s vastness and variegation. Better places to look for hints of trickle-down chiponomics are South Korea, the world leader in advanced memory chips necessary to manage machine-learning workloads, and Taiwan, the global powerhouse in all other AI silicon."},{"index":3,"text":"The AI bonanza is already evident in the two countries’ headline statistics. Swelling exports have lifted annual GDP growth in South Korea to a brisk 4% or so and to a blistering 12% in Taiwan. Private consumption, which was stagnant a year ago, is accelerating (see chart 1). In the second quarter of this year it accounted for around a fifth of the increase in Taiwanese GDP and roughly a third of South Korea’s."},{"index":4,"text":"The economic windfall is most obvious in Taiwan. TSMC, the national champion which manufactures nearly all of the world’s AI chips, increased production from 10m standard silicon wafers in 2019 to 15m last year. Its capital expenditure rose even faster in that period, from $15bn to $41bn. This has helped push up Taiwanese real fixed capital formation by nearly 40% since late 2023."},{"index":5,"text":"Resources are pouring into the semiconductor supply chain. The share of Taiwanese employees making electronic components or computers is nearly 11%, the highest since 2016. Pay rose first in Taiwan’s electronics sector, but since 2024 rises have spread across the workforce. Average nominal wages are now growing at 3% a year in Taiwan, compared with a pre-pandemic trend of 1.9%. Paul Cavey of East Asia Econ, a consultancy, points to the “Balassa-Samuelson effect”, an economic phenomenon whereby high productivity in an industry producing tradable goods bids up the cost of labour across the board."},{"index":6,"text":"The government, for its part, is preparing to spend the swelling tax take from its silicon goose on handouts. Social-welfare spending is set to rise by 40% in 2027. Last month Lai Ching-te, the president, proposed giving all Taiwanese citizens an “AI dividend” of around $300, at a total cost of $7bn (or around 6% of projected revenue). A new child benefit is in the works, too."},{"index":7,"text":"Things are less clear-cut in South Korea. That is chiefly because demand for South Korean silicon increased all of a sudden, rather than gradually. Between 2023 and 2025, as prices of notoriously cyclical memory chips initially fell after a pandemic-era craze for electronics faded, Samsung Electronics, South Korea’s biggest chipmaker, actually trimmed its capital expenditure. SK Hynix, Samsung’s main rival, tripled its spending in that period, but from a relatively piddling $6bn a year."},{"index":8,"text":"The government-backed $500bn investment plan both firms have now signed up to has yet to be reflected in their outlays. In the past three years or so real fixed capital formation has risen by just 3%."},{"index":9,"text":"Most of the past year’s rise in exports has thus come not from higher volumes, as in Taiwan, but from much higher prices. Economy-wide export prices have risen 57% over the past year in South Korea. Immediate benefits are therefore limited to the less than 1% of South Korean workers who toil in the chip industry and the shareholders of the memory duo and their suppliers (plus, indirectly, to the businesses which cater to the lucky few)."},{"index":10,"text":"In nominal terms, average annual wage growth across the economy has fallen below the pre-pandemic pace of 3.5% (see chart 2). The “wealth effect”, whereby a stock-market rally makes share-owning consumers feel flush and spend more, is also muted. Although South Korea’s KOSPI stock-market has more than doubled in value since 2023, a study by South Korea’s central bank estimates that a dollar in equity gains translates to just one extra cent of consumer spending, compared with five cents, plus or minus, for stock-loving American households."},{"index":11,"text":"South Korea’s government is also stingier. Despite projections of a 50% rise in tax revenue in 2027, it is not planning to shower citizens with cash. Some 70% of the windfall will be saved, reckons Capital Economics, a consultancy. The money will mostly be channelled towards deficit reduction and a new Future Response Fund for long-term investments."},{"index":12,"text":"As Samsung and SK Hynix ratchet up their investments, production will increase. This should fuel demand for labour and eventually lift wages for everyone, as happened in Taiwan. Mr Cavey likens South Korea to Australia in 2003-11, when a commodity boom led to a lasting uptick in mining investments that eventually translated into broad economic benefits. The South Korean government’s planned investments in infrastructure and education may also yield productivity gains that Taiwan’s cash handouts would not. The effects of such policies will not be as quick. But they may prove more durable. September 19TH-25TH ｜Finance & economics"}]},{"title":"Actions and words","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.25","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Brain-computer interfaces have come a long way quickly. The first successful decoding of vowel sounds from a neural implant was reported in 2009. In 2021 a team from Edward Chang’s lab at the University of California, San Francisco decoded the words and sentences of a patient who could not speak."},{"index":2,"text":"A new paper by Dr Chang’s group, published in Nature Neuroscience, describes the passing of another milestone. He and his colleagues have enabled two severely paralysed people to communicate with them, by means of an avatar, using both words and gestures. That is an auspicious result for the several million individuals around the world paralysed by strokes, traumatic injuries and degenerative illnesses such as motor-neuron disease."},{"index":3,"text":"The group did this by implanting, through a small incision in the skull of each patient, an array of sensors that covered a large part of the motor cortex—the brain region responsible for controlling bodily motion. This procedure took over two hours, in part because the process for connecting the sensor array to a data-carrying cable is fiddly. But the team are working on a short-cut, using a wireless device, that would reduce this to 40 minutes."},{"index":4,"text":"The implants themselves allowed Dr Chang and his team to collect signals as the patients attempted to say particular words and make particular gestures. They then used those data to train two machine-learning models for each patient—one to decode the words and the other the gestures—allowing them to reconstruct both actions concurrently with approximately two-thirds accuracy. It is the first occasion that such simultaneous decoding has been achieved, allowing patients to communicate more expressively."},{"index":5,"text":"There is further to go. These two initial individuals were restricted to a vocabulary of about ten words and phrases, such as “hello” and “nice to meet you”, and a similar number of gestures, such as a clap or a shrug. But Dr Chang and his collaborators are working on raising this to more than 100 actions. They also hope that the sensor array’s wide reach across the cortex means it may be possible to monitor not only intentions to communicate but also actions intended for the whole body. That might permit paralysed individuals to operate devices such as robots and exoskeletons, which really would be life-changing. September 19TH-25TH ｜Science & technology"}]},{"title":"Crowd control","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.26","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"“This machine is killing me,” croons Phoebe Bridgers in “Lost Boys”, her new song. She does not say whether she has a particular device in mind (or whether, like Rage Against the Machine, she is using the term as a metaphor for the establishment). But, given that her song is about self-destruction and stunted maturity, she may well be referring to a smartphone."},{"index":2,"text":"Elsewhere, however, Ms Bridgers has been loud and clear about her feelings towards the gadgets: she has banned phones during her world tour, which kicked off on September 14th. To gain entry, ticket-holders will have to seal their devices inside pouches that are then magnetically locked for the duration of the gig. That means no filming or taking photos, as fans annoyingly do at other performances. According to Samsung, a phonemaker, each concert-goer in Britain records on average 12 and a half minutes of video per show."},{"index":3,"text":"She is not alone in enforcing such rules. Alicia Keys, Childish Gambino and Jack White have all banned smartphones at their gigs. Sir Paul McCartney barred them at two concerts he played in Los Angeles earlier this year. Bob Dylan’s upcoming tour will be phone-free."},{"index":4,"text":"For some musicians, nostalgia plays a part in their decision. Sir Paul described his LA concerts as “really special”: “It was like an old gig, like how everyone used to play.” But the anti-tech trend is not just a yearning for simpler times. Rather, the hope is that fans will get more out of the live-music experience. The idea is that it is all the more special for being ephemeral."},{"index":5,"text":"Getting your hands on a ticket means getting to enjoy the company of your favourite musician and fellow enthusiasts without a screen in the way. Artists aver that concerts should be appreciated in the moment, not videoed for social media. At Harry Styles’s recent gig in Manchester, guests had to put their phones away, but they were given disposable cameras along with a note: “Thank you for really being here with me. Dance with all your friends.”"},{"index":6,"text":"The effect is scarcity—the only way to experience the show is to be there in person—and that may only heighten fans’ fear of missing out. Musicians have argued that the exclusivity is part of the point. Mr Dylan has said that the fact his performances will be “device-free” will “make the occasion even more unique”."},{"index":7,"text":"Music is meant to connect the performer and the listener. Artists have said that they hate looking out at a sea of iPhones rather than smiling faces. Ghost, a Swedish metal group, banned phones on its tour in 2025; their front man cited the “ever-growing distance I felt between the crowd and the band”. Kaytranada, a Canadian DJ, put it more bluntly: “It looks mad awkward from where I stand.” September 19TH-25TH ｜Culture"}]},{"title":"Not your bond","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.27","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Finance does not offer many sure bets. One, for the past few decades, has been that most rich-world sovereign bonds are safe. Those lending cash to all but the diciest governments could feel confident they would get it back, plus interest at a rate set by a free market, and without the principal being inflated away. Bonds also helpfully offset the volatility of stocks by gaining value during recessions. Today, as public debts mount, all that is under threat."},{"index":2,"text":"Since 2000 net government debt in America and Britain has tripled as a share of GDP. In France and Japan, it has doubled. The pile keeps growing: seldom outside wartime or recessions have rich-world deficits been higher than they are today."},{"index":3,"text":"In the 2010s, when borrowing was cheap, even big debts looked manageable. Today, yields are high and rising fast, most recently in response to the expectation that the war in Iran will force up interest rates. Sure enough on September 16th the Federal Reserve raised rates, becoming the fifth major rich-world central bank to do so this year. In expectation of monetary tightening to come, the ten-year Treasury yield has exceeded 5%, a threshold last crossed for any length of time two decades ago. The spreads on French bonds are not far off their levels during the European sovereign-debt crisis. After lying dormant for decades, yields are surging even in Japan. And the less said about Britain’s gilts, the better."},{"index":4,"text":"In October 2025 The Economist calculated that if America and Britain had to immediately refinance all their debts at prevailing five-year bond yields, they would each need higher taxes or spending cuts worth 2.3% of GDP just to stop debt from rising as a share of the economy. Today, with yields higher, the number in America has more than doubled, to 4.7%. In Britain it has risen to 2.7% even though the government has tightened its belt. France’s adjustment stands at 3.9% of GDP, up from 3.1% a year ago."},{"index":5,"text":"Fortunately, in the real world governments need not refinance everything all at once. However, they have been shifting towards shorter-term debt, meaning that higher interest rates feed through to budgets more quickly than they did. Issuing short is alluring because it is usually cheaper. There is also less demand for long-term debt these days, because the defined-benefit pension funds that have long hungered for it are shrinking. But short-term debt and a volatile bond market make for an inflammable mix."},{"index":6,"text":"It is not yet too late to solve the problem. Like other rich countries, Switzerland has an ageing population. But it is in fine fiscal fettle, with low debt, little inflation and bond yields that have correspondingly stayed low. Motivated governments have cut big deficits before. Sweden vanquished 11% deficits in the 1990s, splitting the job between spending cuts, tax rises and supply-side reforms to boost growth. “Be Swiss or Scandi” may not be realistic advice, but even “Be Greek” would do. Greece has pulled debt-to-GDP down sharply. This summer it issued bonds with lower yields than France’s."},{"index":7,"text":"The trouble is that the next few years’ politics are perilous. In France big-spending populists are looking strong ahead of a presidential election in 2027. In America, which elects a new president in 2028, Democrats and Republicans are vying to promise ever-bigger cheques (literally, in the case of the $5,000 payouts Donald Trump proposed as a reward if Republicans win Congress in the midterms). Britain and Germany, each with its own fiscally lax populists, go to the polls in 2029."},{"index":8,"text":"Politicians may well hope that surging growth from an artificial-intelligence boom will miraculously rescue them from painful budget negotiations. South Korea’s government projects that taxes from chipmaking proceeds will all but eliminate its budget deficit next year. But the productivity growth required to tame deficits is well above what most economists see as plausible, and in any case higher growth also raises interest rates."},{"index":9,"text":"What happens next? Among big debtors only Britain, which faced a market revolt after Liz Truss’s disastrous mini-budget in 2022, has a (semi-serious) plan to tighten. Expect more Truss-like moments elsewhere. Wise governments will respond with sounder budgeting; louche ones with financial shenanigans. Scott Bessent, America’s treasury secretary, began directly intervening in the Treasury market in August in a futile attempt to suppress yields."},{"index":10,"text":"In a recession, more serious trouble could come. It is not inevitable that bond yields fall in downturns. That often fails to happen in emerging markets; and it didn’t in the rich world during the 1970s. Even a smaller-than-usual decline in yields would worsen the economic pain and compound the hit to budgets from higher unemployment. Bond markets, once havens, will have become danger zones. September 19TH-25TH ｜Leaders"}]}]}],"reading":[{"file":"9.21精读(1).pdf","headline":{"title":"French communities fight to keep their","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","series":"考研那些事儿之社论系列"},"paragraphs":[{"index":1,"en":"It is just past noon on a midweek day, and already every table at Au Bon Accueil cafe-bistro in the northern Burgundy village of Saint-Martin-sur-Ouanne is taken.","zh":"一个工作日的午后刚过，位于勃艮第北部圣马丹叙尔瓦讷村的AuBonAccueil咖啡小馆已是座无虚席。"},{"index":2,"en":"The three-course lunch menu – including a choice of a hefty chunk of steak with chips or a meaty lasagne – is just € 18 (£15.50), but villagers are not only here for the food. After an eight-month closure, the bistro, previously called Café de la Gare, has recently reopened – and locals want it to stay that way.“It’s so important to have a cafe-bistro in a village like this. We couldn’t let it stay closed,” said Nadia Letellier, the new manager. “And we’re doing well, as you can see. People seem really happy it’s open again.”","zh":"三道式午餐套餐——可选分量扎实的厚牛排配薯条主，或是肉酱丰富的千层面——价格仅为18欧元（折合15.5英镑）。不过，村民们来到这里，可不只是为了吃饭。家小餐馆此前名为CafédelaGare，歇业了八个月后，最近重新开门营业，当地居民希望它能一直经营下去。“像这样的村子，拥有一家咖啡小馆非常重要。我们不能眼睁睁看着它一直关门。”新任经理娜迪娅·勒泰利耶说道，“而且，正如你所看到的，我们经营得不错。大家似乎都为它重新开业感到高兴。”"},{"index":3,"en":"Hervé Chapuis, a farmer and the village mayor, is part of the local council team that saved Saint-Martin’s last public-facing business since the boulangerie across the road closed eight years ago.“People come here every morning to have a coffee and a chat,” he said. “Our aim is to keep this kind of business open to support the social fabric. It’s part of village life and people missed it. Now, village life has returned to its proper","zh":"埃尔韦·沙皮伊既是农民，也是村长。自从马路对面的面包店八年前关门以来，圣马丹村仅存的这家面向公众营业的商业场所，正是由他所在的地方议会团队出力保住的。“人们每天早上都会来这里喝杯咖啡、聊聊天。”他说道，“我们的目标是让这类商铺继续营业，从而维系社区的社会纽带。它是乡村生活的一部分，大家都很想念它。如今，村里的生活又恢复了原有的节奏。”"},{"index":4,"en":"France’s celebrated cafe-bistros are in trouble. In 1900, there were an estimated 500,000; by the 1960s more than half had closed. Today, only about 30,000-40,000 remain and even they are reportedly shutting at a rate of two a day. Alain Fontaine, who runs Le Mesturet bistro near Paris’ s stock exchange, and who is president of the Bistros and Cafes of France association, said the closures have left rural communities deserted and villagers, especially elderly people, isolated.","zh":"法国久负盛名的咖啡小馆正面临困境。1900年，法国大约有50万家这样的店铺；到20世纪60年代，已有超过一半关门歇业。如今，仅剩约3万至4万家，据报道，它们甚至正以每天两家的速度持续关闭。阿兰·丰泰纳经营着巴黎证卷交易所附近的LeMesturet小酒馆，同时担任法国小酒馆与咖啡馆协会主席。他表示，这些店铺关闭会让乡村社区日渐萧条，也让村民，尤其是老年人，陷入孤立无援的状态。"},{"index":5,"en":"parliaments) are economic and sociological. The number of people living in rural areas has declined over the past half a century, while televisions and other devices have encouraged more people to spend their evenings at home, and the average French person drinks about 80 litres of alcohol a year compared with 200 litres in the 1960s. ləmənt/ The group of elected","zh":"19世纪作家兼政治家莱昂·甘必大曾将这类店铺称为“民主的沙龙”（即“人民的议会”）。其关停原因既有经济层面的，也有社会层面的。过去半个世纪以来，生活在乡村地区的人口不断减少；与此同时，电视和其他电子设备也让越来越多的人习惯晚上待在家里。此外，如今法国人均年酒精消费量约为80升，而20世纪60年代这一数字高达200升。"},{"index":6,"en":"Finding people who are willing to run bars, cafes or bistros is also a problem, according to Fontaine, who is campaigning for the government to submit the establishments to Unesco for intangible cultural heritage listing, arguing this recognition would encourage more people to consider taking them on.","zh":"丰泰纳表示，愿意经营酒吧、咖啡馆或小酒馆的人也很难寻找，这同样是一个难题。他目前正呼吁政府将这类场所申报为联合国教科文组织非物质文化遗产，他认为获得这一认定将鼓励更多人考虑接手经营。"},{"index":7,"en":"“Without a cafe or bistro, communities are in effect deprived of any social life. We cannot resign ourselves to this trend,” he said. “In bistros and cafes, people don’t just eat and drink, they catch up with each other, meet new people, debate, laugh, read, write and sometimes fall in love.","zh":"“没有咖啡馆或小酒馆，社区实际上就失去了社交生活。我们不能对这种趋势听之任之。”他说道，“在小酒馆和咖啡馆里，人们不只是吃饭喝酒，还会叙旧聊天，结识新朋友，就各种话题展开讨论，一起欢笑、阅读、写作，有时甚至还会坠入爱河。”"},{"index":8,"en":"Solène Joucla, whose family have run the historic Le Grand Café at Camplong, near Montpellier, since 1898, faced a choice between having to let it close or giving up her job as an English teacher when her father, Pierre, retired. Instead, she and her sister, Katell, set up an association to run it.“Our aim is to encourage people to go out so they are not isolated, and so we know how they are. People come here for a chat, to have a laugh, listen to music … we are the cultural centre of the village.","zh":"索莱娜·茹克拉的家族自1898年起一直经营着蒙彼利埃附近坎普隆镇历史悠久的LeGrandCafé。当父亲皮埃尔退休后，她面临抉择：要么眼看着咖啡馆关门，要么放弃自己英语教师的工作。最终，她没有让咖啡馆关门，而是和妹妹卡泰尔成立了一个协会来负责经营。“我们的目标是鼓励大家走出家门，这样他们就不会与社会隔绝，我们也能了解他们的生活状况。人们来这里聊聊天、笑一笑、听听音乐……我们就是这个村子的文化中心。”"},{"index":9,"en":"A report submitted to the French senate in 2023 suggested the government should ease regulations on small rural cafe-bistros, hotels and restaurants. It pointed out the negative impact of the widespread closure","zh":"法国参议院于2023年收到的一份报告建议，政府应放宽对乡村小型咖啡小馆、旅店和餐馆的监管。报告指出，这类场所的大量关停对乡村社区产生了负面影响，并得出结论称，这些场所的存在有助于增强“经济活力和社会凝聚力”。"},{"index":10,"en":"The National Federation of Country Bistros said “ rural bistros are unique as places for socialising, building community ties, exploring new experiences and fostering local cultural life. Often, these bistros are the","zh":"法国全国乡村小酒馆联合会表示：“乡村小酒馆是独特的社交场所，人们可以在这里进行社交活动、建立社区联系、体验新鲜事物，并推动当地文化生活的发展。很多时候，这些小酒馆是村庄里仅存的仍在营业的本地服务提场所。”该联合会还表示：“扶持乡村小酒馆，就是维系它的生机，让我们乡村的心脏继续跳动。”"},{"index":11,"en":"In France, the 1,000 Cafés initiative was launched in 2019 to combat the “ social fracture ” highlighted by the gilets jaunes protest movement by helping communes of fewer than 3,500 people save their cafes or open new ones. It found more than 60% of people in rural villages had no businesses locally, and this left many feeling abandoned.","zh":"在法国，“1000家咖啡馆”计划于2019年启动，旨在通过帮助人口不足3500人的市镇保留原有咖啡馆或开设新的咖啡馆以此应对“黄背心”抗议运动所凸显的“社会裂痕”问题。该计划发现，农村村庄中超过60%的人身边没有任何本地商业场所，这让许多人感到自己遭到了遗弃。"},{"index":12,"en":"Camille Sebaux, the director of 1,000 Cafés, told France Info radio the association had worked in more than 300 communities, often helping cafes to diversify by stocking food essentials or becoming drop-off points for online shopping deliveries.“Cafes have been closing in villages for the last 50 years, and we don ’ t have any magic wand or miracle answer,” Sebaux said. “Plus, running a village cafe is not an easy job. Our aim is to help small communities find solutions.”","zh":"“1000家咖啡馆”计划负责人卡米耶·塞博在接受法国资讯广播电台采访时表示，该协会已经在300多个社区开展工作，通常会帮助咖啡馆拓展经营范围，例如增加食品等生活必需品的销售，或者将咖啡馆设为网购商品的取货点，方便居民领取线上购物的配送包裹。“过去50年来，村里的咖啡馆持续关停，我们没有什么魔法棒，也没有什么能解决一切问题的灵丹妙药。”塞博说道，“而且，经营一家乡村咖啡馆并不是件容易的事。我们的目标是帮助小型社区找到解决问题的办法。”"}],"vocab":[{"term":"community","head":"n. A group of people who live in the same area or share common interests, values, or characteristics.","definition":"A group of people who live in the same area or share common interests, values, or characteristics.","gloss":"社区；群体；共同体","example":"The local community has come together to support the new project.","exampleZh":"当地社区的居民齐心协力，支持这项新项目。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"bistro","head":"n. A small, informal restaurant that serves simple meals and drinks.","definition":"A small, informal restaurant that serves simple meals and drinks.","gloss":"小餐馆；小酒馆","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"village","head":"n. A small community or group of houses in a rural area, usually smaller than a town.","definition":"A small community or group of houses in a rural area, usually smaller than a town.","gloss":"村庄；乡村；村民聚居地","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"hefty","head":"a. Large, heavy, or substantial in size, amount, or degree; often implying something considerable or expensive.","definition":"Large, heavy, or substantial in size, amount, or degree; often implying something considerable or expensive.","gloss":"大量的；沉重的；高额的；强有力的","example":"The company was hit with a hefty fine for violating environmental regulations. ti/ Containing a lot of meat; also used","exampleZh":"这家公司因违反环保规定而被处以高额罚款。","tags":[]},{"term":"meaty","head":"a. /ˈmiː to describe something substantial, detailed, or full of important information.","definition":"/ˈmiː to describe something substantial, detailed, or full of important information.","gloss":"肉多的","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"lasagne","head":"n. An Italian dish made with layers of pasta, meat or vegetables, sauce, and cheese, baked in an oven.","definition":"An Italian dish made with layers of pasta, meat or vegetables, sauce, and cheese, baked in an oven.","gloss":"千层面；意式宽面条","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"mayor","head":"n. The elected leader of a city or town, responsible for overseeing local government.","definition":"The elected leader of a city or town, responsible for overseeing local government.","gloss":"市长；镇长","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"council","head":"n. An official group of people elected or appointed to manage the affairs of a town, city, or organization.","definition":"An official group of people elected or appointed to manage the affairs of a town, city, or organization.","gloss":"委员会；理事会；地方议会","example":"The city council voted to build a new public library.","exampleZh":"市议会投票决定修建一座新的公共图书馆。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"boulangerie","head":"n. A French bakery that specializes in making and selling bread, especially baguettes and other traditional breads.","definition":"A French bakery that specializes in making and selling bread, especially baguettes and other traditional breads.","gloss":"法式面包店","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"social fabric","head":"phrase. The relationships, connections, shared values, and institutions that hold a society together.","definition":"The relationships, connections, shared values, and institutions that hold a society together.","gloss":"社会结构","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"stock exchange","head":"phrase. A market where shares of companies and other financial securities are bought and sold.","definition":"A market where shares of companies and other financial securities are bought and sold.","gloss":"证券交易所；股票交易所","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"rural","head":"a. Relating to or characteristic of the countryside, rather than towns or cities.","definition":"Relating to or characteristic of the countryside, rather than towns or cities.","gloss":"农村的；乡村的；田园的","example":"Many rural communities lack access to reliable public transportation.","exampleZh":"许多农村社区缺乏便捷可靠的公共交通服务。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"isolated","head":"a. Separated from other people or places; having little contact or connection with others.","definition":"Separated from other people or places; having little contact or connection with others.","gloss":"偏僻的；孤立的；与世隔绝的","example":"The village remained isolated from the outside world during the severe winter.","exampleZh":"严冬期间，这个村庄与外界隔绝。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"parliament","head":"n. / ˈ p ɑ ː representatives responsible for making and changing the laws of a country.","definition":"/ ˈ p ɑ ː representatives responsible for making and changing the laws of a country.","gloss":"议会；国会；议会制度 tə(r)/ A unit of measurement for liquid","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词 汇","考研大纲词 汇"]},{"term":"litre","head":"n. / ˈ li ː volume, equal to 1,000 millilitres or 1 cubic decimetre.","definition":"/ ˈ li ː volume, equal to 1,000 millilitres or 1 cubic decimetre.","gloss":"升（液体体积单位，等于 1000 毫升） The recipe calls for one litre of water. 这份食谱需要一升 水。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇","考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"campaign for","head":"phrase. To work actively and publicly to support or achieve a particular goal, policy, or change.","definition":"To work actively and publicly to support or achieve a particular goal, policy, or change.","gloss":"为……发起运动；争取；呼吁支持","example":"Many residents are campaigning for better public transportation in their community.","exampleZh":"许多居民正在发起运动，呼吁改善社区的公共交通。","tags":[]},{"term":"establishment","head":"n. The act of creating or setting up something; an organization, institution, or business; the process of becoming accepted or firmly established.","definition":"The act of creating or setting up something; an organization, institution, or business; the process of becoming accepted or firmly established.","gloss":"机构；确立","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"intangible","head":"a. Unable to be touched or physically measured; relating to things such as ideas, feelings, or qualities rather than physical objects.","definition":"Unable to be touched or physically measured; relating to things such as ideas, feelings, or qualities rather than physical objects.","gloss":"无形的；难以触及的；非物质的","example":"Trust is an intangible asset that plays an important role in building strong relationships.","exampleZh":"信任是一种无形资产，在建立牢固的人际关系中发挥着重要作用。","tags":[]},{"term":"be deprived of","head":"phrase. To be prevented from having or using something important or necessary, often against one's will.","definition":"To be prevented from having or using something important or necessary, often against one's will.","gloss":"被剥夺；被迫失去；无法享有","example":"Children in rural areas should not be deprived of access to quality education.","exampleZh":"农村地区的儿童不应被剥夺接受优质教育的机会。","tags":[]},{"term":"catch up with","head":"phrase. To reach the same level or standard as someone or something; to achieve the same progress after being behind.","definition":"To reach the same level or standard as someone or something; to achieve the same progress after being behind.","gloss":"赶上；追上；达到同等水平","example":"The smaller towns are gradually catching up with major cities in terms of technological development.","exampleZh":"在技术发展方面，中小城镇正逐渐赶上大城市。","tags":[]},{"term":"give up","head":"phrase. To stop trying to do something; to abandon an activity, habit, or attempt.","definition":"To stop trying to do something; to abandon an activity, habit, or attempt.","gloss":"放弃；停止；戒除","example":"She refused to give up her dream of becoming a teacher.","exampleZh":"她拒绝放弃成为一名教师的梦想。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"association","head":"n. A connection between people, things, or ideas; an organization formed by people who share a common purpose or interest.","definition":"A connection between people, things, or ideas; an organization formed by people who share a common purpose or interest.","gloss":"协会","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"encourage","head":"v. To give someone support, confidence, or hope; to persuade someone to do something by making it seem attractive or worthwhile.","definition":"To give someone support, confidence, or hope; to persuade someone to do something by making it seem attractive or worthwhile.","gloss":"鼓励；支持；促进；劝说","example":"Teachers should encourage students to think independently and express their opinions.","exampleZh":"师应鼓励学生独立思考并表达自己的观点。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"chat","head":"n. To talk to someone in a friendly, informal way, usually about things that are not serious.","definition":"To talk to someone in a friendly, informal way, usually about things that are not serious.","gloss":"闲聊；聊天；交谈","example":"She spent the evening chatting with her friends online.","exampleZh":"她整个晚上都在网上和朋友聊天。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"vitality","head":"n. The energy, enthusiasm, and strength that a person, organization, or activity possesses.","definition":"The energy, enthusiasm, and strength that a person, organization, or activity possesses.","gloss":"活力；生命力；生机","example":"The vitality of young people brings new energy to society.","exampleZh":"年轻人的活力为社会注入了新的能量。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"cohesion","head":"n. The state of being united and working together effectively; the way in which parts of something are closely connected.","definition":"The state of being united and working together effectively; the way in which parts of something are closely connected.","gloss":"凝聚力；团结；衔接；连贯性","example":"Social cohesion is essential for maintaining a stable and harmonious society.","exampleZh":"社会凝聚力对于维护稳定和谐的社会至关重要。","tags":["非考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"provider","head":"n. A person, company, or organization that supplies goods, services, or support.","definition":"A person, company, or organization that supplies goods, services, or support.","gloss":"提供者；供应商；服务提供商","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"countryside","head":"n. Land outside towns and cities, especially land used for farming or covered with natural scenery. people enjoy spending their weekends in the countryside.","definition":"Land outside towns and cities, especially land used for farming or covered with natural scenery. people enjoy spending their weekends in the countryside.","gloss":"乡村；农村；乡间 Many许多人喜欢在乡村度过周末。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"gilets jaunes","head":"phrase. =","definition":"=","gloss":"该短语是法语：Gilets jaunes 是法语，直译是：黄色背心 / 黄背心，gilet = 马甲、背心，jaune黄色的，如果是在法国政治/社会新闻里，les Gilets jaunes特指法国的 “黄背心运动”，2018 年前后兴起的一场社会抗议运动。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"combat","head":"v. (v.) To try to stop or reduce something harmful or undesirable; (n.) Fighting between opposing forces.","definition":"(v.) To try to stop or reduce something harmful or undesirable; (n.) Fighting between opposing forces.","gloss":"与……作斗争；防止；战斗；斗争","example":"The government has introduced new measures to combat air pollution.","exampleZh":"政府出台了新措施，以治理空气污染。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"protest","head":"n. To express strong disagreement with or opposition to something; (n.) An organized public demonstration against something.","definition":"To express strong disagreement with or opposition to something; (n.) An organized public demonstration against something.","gloss":"抗议；抗议活动","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"abandon","head":"v. /ə ˈ bændən/ To leave someone or something behind permanently, especially when it is difficult to continue supporting or caring for them.","definition":"/ə ˈ bændən/ To leave someone or something behind permanently, especially when it is difficult to continue supporting or caring for them.","gloss":"放弃；抛弃；遗弃","example":"The company decided to abandon the plan because of rising costs.","exampleZh":"由于成本不断上升，公司决定放弃这项计划。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"diversify","head":"v. To develop a wider range of products, services, or activities in order to reduce dependence on one particular area.","definition":"To develop a wider range of products, services, or activities in order to reduce dependence on one particular area.","gloss":"使多样化；使多元化；分散风险","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"essential","head":"a. /ɪ ˈ senʃəl/ Completely necessary or extremely important for a particular purpose or situation.","definition":"/ɪ ˈ senʃəl/ Completely necessary or extremely important for a particular purpose or situation.","gloss":"必不可少的；必要的；极其重要的【考研大纲词汇】","example":"Regular exercise is essential for maintaining good health.","exampleZh":"定期锻炼对于保持身体健康至关重要。","tags":["考研大纲词 汇"]},{"term":"drop off","head":"phrase. off to take someone or something to a place by car and leave them there on your way to another place I'll drop you off on my way home.","definition":"off to take someone or something to a place by car and leave them there on your way to another place I'll drop you off on my way home.","gloss":"顺便把某人/某物送到某处我回家路上顺道送你吧。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"delivery","head":"n. The act of taking goods, letters, or other items to a particular person or place; the process of providing something.","definition":"The act of taking goods, letters, or other items to a particular person or place; the process of providing something.","gloss":"递送；交付；运送；提供","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"wand","head":"n. a thin stick you hold in your hand to do magic tricks〔","definition":"a thin stick you hold in your hand to do magic tricks〔","gloss":"表演魔术用的〕魔杖","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]}]}],"tests":[{"file":"【福利】9.21 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","type":"reading","instructions":[],"passage":[],"items":[{"number":21,"stem":"The author describes the busy reopening of Au Bon Accueil in Paragraphs 1 and 2 in order to _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"show that a reopened village bistro has regained its role as a busy social centre"},{"key":"B","text":"prove that inexpensive lunch menus alone can rescue every struggling rural cafe-bistro"},{"key":"C","text":"suggest that the closure of the village boulangerie made the bistro less important"},{"key":"D","text":"explain why French bistros have never been able to survive outside large cities"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"[A]","analysis":["【题型】例证题·开篇场景功能","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraphs 1-2","【解析】","- 原文（Para.1）：It is just past noon on a midweek day, and already every table at Au Bon Accueil cafe-bistro in the northern Burgundy village of Saint-Martin-sur-Ouanne is taken.","- 原文（Para.2）：After an eight-month closure, the bistro, previously called Café de la Gare, has recently reopened – and locals want it to stay that way.","- 原文（Para.2）：“It’s so important to have a cafe-bistro in a village like this. We couldn’t let it stay closed,” said Nadia Letellier, the new manager.","分析：","- 选项A（正确）：开篇用午间满座和 manager 的评价证明小酒馆重新成为村民聚集的社交中心，例子服务于全文关于乡村 bistro 社会功能的主题。","- 选项B（错误）：过度推理陷阱。€18 午餐只是场景细节，不能推出便宜菜单足以拯救所有乡村 bistro。","- 选项C（错误）：反向细节陷阱。boulangerie 关闭使 bistro 成为村里最后一个对外营业场所，因此更重要而非不重要。","- 选项D（错误）：无中生有陷阱。开篇没有讨论大城市生存优势，后文反而强调乡村社区的需要。","【命题技巧】","- 场景-观点关联：满座场景与 locals want it to stay that way 共同引出社区价值。","- 细节到主题升级：午餐价格只是背景，正确答案概括例子服务的社会功能。"]},{"number":22,"stem":"According to Paragraph 4, France's celebrated cafe-bistros have _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"expanded from about 30,000 to more than 500,000 over the past century"},{"key":"B","text":"stabilised because rural communities have recently attracted many new residents"},{"key":"C","text":"survived mainly because local councils bought them for public ownership"},{"key":"D","text":"suffered a long-term contraction that continues to threaten community life"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"[D]","analysis":["【题型】细节理解题·数据与影响识别","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 4","【解析】","- 原文（Para.4）：In 1900, there were an estimated 500,000; by the 1960s more than half had closed. Today, only about 30,000-40,000 remain and even they are reportedly shutting at a rate of two a day.","- 原文（Para.4）：Alain Fontaine... said the closures have left rural communities deserted and villagers, especially elderly people, isolated.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：数字反向陷阱。原文数量从 500,000 降至 30,000-40,000，不是扩张。","- 选项B（错误）：无中生有陷阱。第 5 段说乡村人口下降，本段没有稳定或回流信息。","- 选项C（错误）：细节错位陷阱。后文有个别社区自救，但第 4 段没有说地方政府普遍收购。","- 选项D（正确）：长期数量下降、每天约两家关闭，以及乡村社区荒废和老人孤立，共同说明持续萎缩带来社区影响。","【命题技巧】","- 时间轴数据定位：1900、1960s、today 构成明显下降链条。","- 数字到社会后果串联：closures 与 deserted、isolated 建立因果联系。"]},{"number":23,"stem":"The phrase “salons de la démocratie” (Paragraph 5) suggests that bistros function as _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"profitable tourist attractions that advertise regional food and wine"},{"key":"B","text":"official committee rooms controlled by local government officials"},{"key":"C","text":"informal public forums where villagers can meet and exchange views"},{"key":"D","text":"private clubs reserved for merchants and nineteenth-century writers"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"[C]","analysis":["【题型】词义猜测题·文化短语语境理解","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 5","【解析】","- 原文（Para.5）：The reasons for the closures of what the 19th-century writer and statesman Léon Gambetta called “salons de la démocratie” (people’s parliaments) are economic and sociological.","- 原文（Para.7）：In bistros and cafes, people don’t just eat and drink, they catch up with each other, meet new people, debate, laugh, read, write and sometimes fall in love.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：概念偷换陷阱。短语强调民主社交空间，不是旅游盈利属性。","- 选项B（错误）：正式性偷换陷阱。people’s parliaments 是比喻，不能理解为政府控制的委员会。","- 选项C（正确）：括号中的 people’s parliaments 以及后文 debate、meet new people 表明 bistro 是非正式公共讨论和交往场所。","- 选项D（错误）：范围偷换陷阱。bistro 面向村民而非特定商人或作家群体，也不是私人俱乐部。","【命题技巧】","- 释义线索定位：people’s parliaments 直接解释法语短语。","- 上下文互证：第 7 段的 debate 与 exchange views 形成语义呼应。"]},{"number":24,"stem":"It can be inferred from Paragraphs 8-12 that saving rural bistros often depends on _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"strict compliance with existing regulations that already protect small businesses"},{"key":"B","text":"local associations and flexible services that meet practical community needs"},{"key":"C","text":"UNESCO recognition that has already guaranteed a new generation of managers"},{"key":"D","text":"younger customers who refuse to use bistros for deliveries or entertainment"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"[B]","analysis":["【题型】推理判断题·解决措施综合推理","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraphs 8-12","【解析】","- 原文（Para.8）：Instead, she and her sister, Katell, set up an association to run it.","- 原文（Para.9）：A report submitted to the French senate in 2023 suggested the government should ease regulations on small rural cafe-bistros, hotels and restaurants.","- 原文（Para.12）：...often helping cafes to diversify by stocking food essentials or becoming drop-off points for online shopping deliveries.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：反向政策陷阱。报告建议 ease regulations，而不是严格执行既有监管。","- 选项B（正确）：姐妹成立协会经营、1,000 Cafés 协助社区寻找方案，以及 stocking essentials、delivery drop-offs 等多元化服务，可推知社区组织和灵活服务很重要。","- 选项C（错误）：时态过度陷阱。UNESCO listing 只是 Fontaine 希望政府提交的方案，并未保证新经营者出现。","- 选项D（错误）：反向细节陷阱。bistro 可承担配送、音乐等活动，说明功能扩展，不是年轻顾客拒绝使用。","【命题技巧】","- 多例归纳推理：association、diversify、drop-off points 共同指向社区化灵活方案。","- 政策语气识别：ease regulations 与 strict compliance 形成反向干扰。"]},{"number":25,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Cheap Lunches: Why French Rural Cuisine Is Losing Customers"},{"key":"B","text":"UNESCO Recognition: The Only Way to Save France's Cafe-Bistros"},{"key":"C","text":"Keeping the Heart Beating: French Villages Fight for Their Bistros"},{"key":"D","text":"Last Orders: Why Rural France Can No Longer Support Local Business"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"[C]","analysis":["【题型】主旨大意题·最佳标题","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Full text","【解析】","- 原文（Para.3）：Our aim is to keep this kind of business open to support the social fabric.","- 原文（Para.10）：Supporting the village bistro means helping to keep it alive, so the heart of our countryside continues to beat.","- 原文（Para.12）：Cafes have been closing in villages for the last 50 years, and we don’t have any magic wand or miracle answer... Our aim is to help small communities find solutions.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：以偏概全陷阱。文章不是美食或价格竞争分析，而是 bistro 对乡村社交与社区纽带的意义。","- 选项B（错误）：细节扩大陷阱。UNESCO 只是第 6 段的一种倡导，不是全文唯一解决方案。","- 选项C（正确）：French villages 对应各类社区行动，bistros 点明对象，keeping the heart beating 呼应社会凝聚力和乡村心跳的比喻，能覆盖拯救行动、原因分析和多元方案。","- 选项D（错误）：反向主旨陷阱。文章确实描述衰退，但重点是社区和机构正在寻找办法，不是宣告无力支持。","【命题技巧】","- 比喻主线整合：social fabric 与 heart of our countryside 都指向社区存续。","- 标题范围校准：正确标题同时包含危机与行动，避免只覆盖某个建议。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语阅读理解大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。","如果对答案解析有疑惑，直接打开任何AI大模型（如Deepseek、Kimi等），上传该篇原文文档，然后复制粘贴对应的题目和选项，说清楚你的疑问，大模型会回应解释对应的问题。"]}]},{"file":"【福利】9.21检验题__完型填空(1).docx","type":"cloze","instructions":["Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)"],"passage":["France’s celebrated cafe-bistros are in trouble. In 1900, there were an estimated 500,000; by the 1960s more than half had ____【11】____. Today, only about 30,000-40,000 remain and even they are reportedly shutting at a ____【12】____ of two a day. Alain Fontaine, who runs Le Mesturet bistro near Paris’s stock exchange, and who is president of the Bistros and Cafes of France association, said the closures have left rural communities ____【13】____ and villagers, especially elderly people, ____【14】____.","The reasons for the closures of what the 19th-century writer and statesman Léon Gambetta called “salons de la démocratie” (people’s parliaments) are economic and ____【15】____. The number of people living in rural areas has declined over the past half a century, while televisions and other devices have ____【16】____ more people to spend their evenings at home, and the average French person drinks about 80 litres of alcohol a year compared with 200 litres in the 1960s.","Finding people who are willing to ____【17】____ bars, cafes or bistros is also a problem, according to Fontaine, who is campaigning for the government to ____【18】____ the establishments to Unesco for intangible cultural heritage ____【19】____, arguing this recognition would ____【20】____ more people to consider taking them on."],"items":[{"number":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"opened"},{"key":"B","text":"survived"},{"key":"C","text":"closed"},{"key":"D","text":"moved"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (closed)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·趋势语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第2句","【解析】","[A] opened（开业）：与 in trouble 和 closures 相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] survived（存续）：与 more than half 后的衰落语境不合。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] closed（关闭）：more than half had closed 指超过半数已经关闭。正确答案。","[D] moved（搬迁）：不是地点迁移问题。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"pace"},{"key":"B","text":"level"},{"key":"C","text":"cost"},{"key":"D","text":"rate"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (rate)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·固定搭配","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第3句","【解析】","[A] pace（节奏）：可指速度，但 shutting at a rate of 更强调单位时间关闭数量。近义干扰。","[B] level（水平）：不能表示“每天两家”的频率。语义场干扰。","[C] cost（成本）：语义偏移。语义场干扰。","[D] rate（速率）：at a rate of two a day 指以每天两家的速度关闭。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"crowded"},{"key":"B","text":"deserted"},{"key":"C","text":"modernised"},{"key":"D","text":"divided"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (deserted)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·社区状态","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第5句","【解析】","[A] crowded（拥挤的）：与乡村小店关闭后的空寂相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] deserted（被遗弃的、空荡的）：left rural communities deserted 指乡村社区变得冷清。正确答案。","[C] modernised（现代化的）：不是现代化结果。语义场干扰。","[D] divided（分裂的）：语义过重且不符。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"isolated"},{"key":"B","text":"employed"},{"key":"C","text":"welcomed"},{"key":"D","text":"protected"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (isolated)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·社会后果","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第5句","【解析】","[A] isolated（孤立的）：elderly people isolated 指老年人被隔绝。正确答案。","[B] employed（受雇的）：不是就业影响。语义场干扰。","[C] welcomed（受欢迎的）：方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] protected（受保护的）：与缺乏社交场所的后果相反。逻辑方向干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"political"},{"key":"B","text":"cultural"},{"key":"C","text":"sociological"},{"key":"D","text":"technological"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (sociological)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·成因分类","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第1句","【解析】","[A] political（政治的）：后文讲人口、居家习惯和饮酒量变化。语义场干扰。","[B] cultural（文化的）：外延过大。上下义干扰。","[C] sociological（社会学的）：人口变化、社交习惯与饮酒行为属于社会性原因。正确答案。","[D] technological（技术的）：电视机只是媒介，不是技术产业分析。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"encouraged"},{"key":"B","text":"required"},{"key":"C","text":"forbidden"},{"key":"D","text":"reminded"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (encouraged)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·因果行为","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第2句","【解析】","[A] encouraged（鼓励）：电视等设备促使更多人待在家里。正确答案。","[B] required（要求）：设备没有强制力。程度差异干扰。","[C] forbidden（禁止）：方向和语义均不合。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] reminded（提醒）：不构成行为诱因。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"visit"},{"key":"B","text":"finance"},{"key":"C","text":"protect"},{"key":"D","text":"run"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (run)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·经营语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第1句","【解析】","[A] visit（拜访）：问题是找不到经营者，不是找不到顾客。语义场干扰。","[B] finance（资助）：后文才谈遗产认定带来的吸引力。语义场干扰。","[C] protect（保护）：语境重点是实际经营。近义干扰。","[D] run（经营）：run bars, cafes or bistros 指经营这些场所。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"compare"},{"key":"B","text":"submit"},{"key":"C","text":"restrict"},{"key":"D","text":"sell"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (submit)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·官方动作","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第2句","【解析】","[A] compare（比较）：不与 to Unesco 构成申报动作。搭配干扰。","[B] submit（提交）：submit... to Unesco 指向联合国教科文组织申报。正确答案。","[C] restrict（限制）：与争取文化认可相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] sell（出售）：语义无关。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"listing"},{"key":"B","text":"licence"},{"key":"C","text":"training"},{"key":"D","text":"audit"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (listing)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·制度语义","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第2句","【解析】","[A] listing（列入名录）：heritage listing 指列入文化遗产名录。正确答案。","[B] licence（执照）：不是经营许可。语义场干扰。","[C] training（培训）：未涉及培训项目。语义场干扰。","[D] audit（审计）：不是审查评估。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"force"},{"key":"B","text":"discourage"},{"key":"C","text":"encourage"},{"key":"D","text":"qualify"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (encourage)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·结果关系","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第2句","【解析】","[A] force（强迫）：认可产生的是吸引而非强制。感情色彩干扰。","[B] discourage（使气馁）：方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] encourage（鼓励）：would encourage more people to consider... 指鼓励更多人考虑接手经营。正确答案。","[D] qualify（使合格）：认可不直接使人获得资格。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语完型填空大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。"]}]},{"file":"【福利】9.21检验题__新题型__7选5(1).docx","type":"gap-fill","instructions":["Directions:"],"passage":["Part B","In the following text, some sentences have been removed. For Questions 41-45, choose the most suitable one from the list A-G to fit into each of the numbered blanks. There are two extra choices, which do not fit in any of the blanks. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","It is just past noon on a midweek day, and already every table at Au Bon Accueil cafe-bistro in the northern Burgundy village of Saint-Martin-sur-Ouanne is taken.","The three-course lunch menu – including a choice of a hefty chunk of steak with chips or a meaty lasagne – is just €18 (£15.50), but villagers are not only here for the food. After an eight-month closure, the bistro, previously called Café de la Gare, has recently reopened – and locals want it to stay that way. (41)","Hervé Chapuis, a farmer and the village mayor, is part of the local council team that saved Saint-Martin’s last public-facing business since the boulangerie across the road closed eight years ago. (42)","France’s celebrated cafe-bistros are in trouble. In 1900, there were an estimated 500,000; by the 1960s more than half had closed. Today, only about 30,000-40,000 remain and even they are reportedly shutting at a rate of two a day. (43)","The reasons for the closures of what the 19th-century writer and statesman Léon Gambetta called “salons de la démocratie” (people’s parliaments) are economic and sociological. (44)","Finding people who are willing to run bars, cafes or bistros is also a problem, according to Fontaine, who is campaigning for the government to submit the establishments to Unesco for intangible cultural heritage listing, arguing this recognition would encourage more people to consider taking them on. (45)","Solène Joucla, whose family have run the historic Le Grand Café at Camplong, near Montpellier, since 1898, faced a choice between having to let it close or giving up her job as an English teacher when her father, Pierre, retired. Instead, she and her sister, Katell, set up an association to run it. “Our aim is to encourage people to go out so they are not isolated, and so we know how they are. People come here for a chat, to have a laugh, listen to music … we are the cultural centre of the village.","[A] Alain Fontaine, president of the Bistros and Cafes of France association, said the closures have left rural communities deserted and villagers, especially elderly people, isolated.","[B] “Without a cafe or bistro, communities are in effect deprived of any social life. We cannot resign ourselves to this trend. In bistros and cafes, people don’t just eat and drink, they catch up with each other, meet new people, debate, laugh, read, write and sometimes fall in love.”","[C] “It’s so important to have a cafe-bistro in a village like this. We couldn’t let it stay closed. And we’re doing well, as you can see. People seem really happy it’s open again.”","[D] The number of people living in rural areas has declined over the past half a century, while televisions and other devices have encouraged more people to spend their evenings at home, and the average French person drinks about 80 litres of alcohol a year compared with 200 litres in the 1960s.","[E] A report submitted to the French senate suggested the government should ease regulations on small rural cafe-bistros, hotels and restaurants.","[F] “People come here every morning to have a coffee and a chat. Our aim is to keep this kind of business open to support the social fabric. It’s part of village life and people missed it. Now, village life has returned to its proper rhythm.”","[G] Supporting the village bistro means helping to keep it alive, so the heart of our countryside continues to beat."],"items":[{"number":41,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"C","kicker":"【答案】C","analysis":["【定位】第二段段末；第三段开始写镇长和村委会。","【解析】空前说村民来酒馆不只是为了食物，并交代酒馆重开；【C】中 happy it’s open again 直接承接重开，而 village like this 又把话题引向乡村公共生活。第三段的 mayor、social fabric 和 village life 正是在解释这种情感背后的公共价值。","逐项分析"]},{"number":42,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"F","kicker":"【答案】F","analysis":["【定位】第三段段末；第四段转向全国衰落。","【解析】空前说 Chapuis 和村委会拯救了村里最后一家面向公众的业务；【F】开头的 People come here every morning 回应这种公共性，末句 village life has returned to its proper rhythm 收束地方复兴。第四段用 France’s…are in trouble 制造由局部成功到全国危机的转折。","逐项分析"]},{"number":43,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"A","kicker":"【答案】A","analysis":["【定位】第四段段末；第五段分析关闭原因。","【解析】前文给出 1900 年、1960 年代和今天的数据，说明 cafe-bistro 持续减少；【A】中的 closures 承接 shutting，并把影响落到 rural communities 与 elderly people。第五段首句 The reasons for the closures 随即转入原因分析，形成“现象—后果—原因”的推进。","逐项分析"]},{"number":44,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"D","kicker":"【答案】D","analysis":["【定位】第五段段末；第六段补充经营者问题。","【解析】空前说关闭原因包括经济和社会两类；【D】具体展开人口减少、居家娱乐和饮酒量下降三类变化，正是 sociological 与 economic 的证据。第六段 Finding people…is also a problem 用 also 表明又在补充另一类原因。","逐项分析"]},{"number":45,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"B","kicker":"【答案】B","analysis":["【定位】第六段段末；第七段转入 Solène 的自救案例。","【解析】空前说 Fontaine 推动 Unesco 非物质文化遗产认定，希望吸引更多人接手；【B】先说不能接受社区失去社会生活，再列出酒馆中的聊天、相遇、辩论和阅读，解释其文化价值。第七段 Solène 设立协会经营家族酒馆，正是这种价值促使地方自救的实例。","逐项分析","干扰项设置","【E】利用 government 和 regulations 设置政策方案诱饵，但本篇主体是社会功能与地方自救；【G】复现 village bistro 和 countryside，态度鲜明，却缺少可验证的上下文衔接，属于总结性口号干扰。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语新题型大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。目前是在测试阶段，该题型仅提供到12月底。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。"]}]}],"qa":[],"originals":[{"kind":"source","label":"原文","file":"9.21—9.27 原文(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":157055,"date":"2026-09-21","range":"2026-09-21|2026-09-27","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.21—9.27 原文(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"source","label":"原文","file":"9.21—9.27 原文(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":398345,"date":"2026-09-21","range":"2026-09-21|2026-09-27","title":"French communities fight to keep their","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.21—9.27 原文(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"reading","label":"精读","file":"9.21精读(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":735199,"date":"2026-09-21","range":"","title":"French communities fight to keep their","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.21精读(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.21 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":57007,"date":"2026-09-21","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.21 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.21检验题__完型填空(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":59529,"date":"2026-09-21","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.21检验题__完型填空(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.21检验题__新题型__7选5(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":78056,"date":"2026-09-21","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.21检验题__新题型__7选5(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"layout","label":"杂志排版","file":"9.21杂志排版(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":768913,"date":"2026-09-21","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.21杂志排版(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"}]};
